{
  "dom_id": "step-q344453",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14095797/assets/4ae6f37660956d6d7015289c4a185c12ef4ab39f84e9cff394f534b14854c5cd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are decreasing in length at a rate of $3\\text{\u00a0m/min}.$ At what rate is the area of \u200b\u200bthe square decreasing when the side is $12\\,\\text{m}$ long?\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{m/min},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\cdot 2(12)=-72{\\text{\u00a0m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is decreasing at $72\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
